  "narrative": "All returned estimates are signed to the registered claim direction. For these positive-direction conditional claims, a claim is supported only when the point estimate and the lower end of the uncertainty interval indicate a difference exceeding the 0.15-unit threshold in the claimed direction. The robustness results are strong for the main TP53-loss and KEAP1-loss associations. In cells without KEAP1 loss, TP53 loss remains associated with a higher dependency_composite by 0.367 units, with lower 95% bound 0.265 (H16). Conversely, in cells without TP53 loss, KEAP1 loss remains associated with a higher dependency_composite by 0.780 units, lower bound 0.719 (H17). Thus each alteration is not merely a proxy for the other, although the conditional intervals still overlap substantially and do not establish independence or causality.\n\nThe effects also remain above the threshold within the dominant Avana library: TP53-loss effect 0.429, lower bound 0.327 (H18), and KEAP1-loss effect 0.814, lower bound 0.743 (H19). They remain above the threshold within the dominant adenocarcinoma subtype: TP53-loss 0.414, lower bound 0.301 (H21); KEAP1-loss 0.814, lower bound 0.734 (H22). They also persist in the largest screen batch: TP53-loss 0.456, lower bound 0.271 (H23); KEAP1-loss 0.733, lower bound 0.595 (H24). These conditional acceptances are not independent confirmations because they use overlapping samples from the same dataset, but they make simple explanations such as complete co-mutation dependence, complete Avana-library confinement, adenocarcinoma-only confinement, or concentration in batch_a less plausible for the average dataset associations.\n\nThe interaction results are more fragile. H20 suggests that KEAP1 loss may have a stronger association when TP53 loss is present, with signed estimate 0.210, but the lower bound is 0.019. Although the interval excludes zero, it is compatible with effects far below the 0.15 threshold, and the exposed-subgroup cell is only 85 observations. I therefore reject it as a supported meaningful interaction rather than accept a borderline interaction under multiplicity. H25 does not support a larger TP53 effect in Avana versus non-Avana (estimate 0.063, interval -0.256 to 0.382). H26 has a negative signed estimate, -0.166, so it opposes the claimed positive Avana interaction; however, reversing the claim would still not be supported because the reversed interval would cross zero and not robustly exceed the threshold. H27 does not support a larger KEAP1 effect when research_signature_a is non-negative (estimate 0.070, interval -0.061 to 0.200).\n\nOverall, KEAP1 loss is the strongest and most stable dataset-specific measured predictor of higher dependency_composite. TP53 loss is also robustly associated, but has an accepted large heterogeneity signal with research_signature_a (H15), so it should be reported as an average dataset association with context dependence rather than a uniform predictor. No causal interpretation is implied. I request independent validation for H14, the KEAP1 main effect, because it has the largest magnitude and every direct conditional lower bound tested so far remains well above the threshold. This is a focused validation of the strongest claim, not of the constructed signature interaction or of the weaker borderline KEAP1-by-TP53 interaction.",
  "research_notes": "Current accepted dataset-specific claims include TP53 loss -> higher dependency_composite, KEAP1 loss -> higher dependency_composite, and TP53-by-research_signature_a heterogeneity. Discovery robustness now supports accepted conditional effects for TP53/KEAP1 in the opposite alteration context, Avana library, adenocarcinoma, and batch_a. Rejected or not meaningful: KEAP1-by-TP53 interaction H20; TP53 and KEAP1 Avana-library interactions H25/H26; KEAP1-by-research_signature_a interaction H27. KEAP1 is strongest, with lower bounds at least 0.595 across conditional tests. TP53 remains robust but is heterogeneous by research_signature_a.\n\nRequested validation: H14 KEAP1 main effect. When validation arrives, reassess H14 explicitly. If independent estimate remains above 0.15, report KEAP1 loss as a strongly supported supplied-dataset association. If independent lower bound falls below 0.15 or the estimate collapses, keep H14 accepted only for the original discovery data but downgrade the general supplied-dataset interpretation and revisit conditional claims. If validation unexpectedly reverses, reconsider H14 and possibly related conditionals.\n\nNext analysis priority: 1) H28/H29 to see whether TP53 and KEAP1 effects differ by research_signature_b; 2) H30-H33 broad loss screens (SMARCA4, ARID1A, CDKN2A, MTAP); 3) newly registered non-Avana conditional main effects for KEAP1 and TP53. Avoid validation of H15 or H20 unless a much stronger pattern emerges, because H15 is an arbitrary-signature interaction and H20 is interval-imprecise for the threshold. Keep all conclusions noncausal and dataset-specific; the composite endpoint does not identify a single-gene dependency mechanism.",
